|> Bootcamp
    title=Components Playground
    handle=playground
    page-necessary-margin=270
    max-element-width=1200


    |> Exercises
        |> Exercise
            handle=grid-component

            |> ExerciseStatement
                What is the Grid component ?
            |> Solution
                _*Grid*_ used for putting items in a controlled grid layout, a more friendly alternative to Table component that needs all special html tags tr, colgroup ...etc

                |> Pause

                _*Case 1:*_ 3 cols, 2 on mobile, place-items="start"

                |> Grid
                    cols=3
                    mobile-cols=2
                    place-items=start

                    Item 1

                    Item 2

                    Item 3

                    Item 4

                    Item 5

                    Item 6

                |> Pause

                _*Case 2:*_ 3 cols, place-items="center" (default)

                |> Grid
                    cols=3

                    Short

                    Medium text

                    A longer piece of text

                    Item 4

                    Item 5

                    Item 6

                |> Pause

                _*Case 3:*_ 3 cols, place-items="end"

                |> Grid
                    cols=3
                    place-items=end

                    Short

                    Medium text

                    A longer piece of text

                    Item 4

                    Item 5

                    Item 6

                |> Pause

                _*Case 4:*_ 2 cols, column-first=true (fills top-to-bottom)

                |> Grid
                    cols=2
                    column-first=true
                    place-items=start

                    1st

                    2nd

                    3rd

                    4th

                    5th

                    6th

                |> Pause

                _*Case 5:*_ 2 cols, column-first=false (default, fills left-to-right)

                |> Grid
                    cols=2
                    place-items=start

                    1st

                    2nd

                    3rd

                    4th

                    5th

                    6th

                |> Pause

                _*Case 6:*_ with-padding=false

                |> Grid
                    cols=3
                    with-padding=false
                    place-items=start

                    Item 1

                    Item 2

                    Item 3

                    Item 4

                    Item 5

                    Item 6

                |> Pause

                _*Case 7:*_ custom gap="2rem"

                |> Grid
                    cols=3
                    gap=2rem
                    place-items=start

                    Item 1

                    Item 2

                    Item 3

                    Item 4

                    Item 5

                    Item 6

                |> Pause

                _*Case 8:*_ custom gap="0.25rem"

                |> Grid
                    cols=3
                    gap=0.25rem
                    place-items=start

                    Item 1

                    Item 2

                    Item 3

                    Item 4

                    Item 5

                    Item 6

                |> Pause

                _*Case 9:*_ margin-top=40, margin-bottom=40

                |> Grid
                    cols=3
                    margin-top=40
                    margin-bottom=40
                    place-items=start

                    Item 1

                    Item 2

                    Item 3

                    Item 4

                    Item 5

                    Item 6

                |> Pause

                _*Case 10:*_ custom mobile-cutoff=800 (switches to mobile layout at 800px)

                |> Grid
                    cols=4
                    mobile-cols=2
                    mobile-cutoff=800
                    place-items=start

                    Item 1

                    Item 2

                    Item 3

                    Item 4

                    Item 5

                    Item 6

                    Item 7

                    Item 8

                |> Pause

                _*Case 11:*_ overflow row (5 items in 3 cols)

                |> Grid
                    cols=3
                    place-items=start

                    Item 1

                    Item 2

                    Item 3

                    Item 4

                    Item 5

                |> Pause

                _*Case 12:*_ column-first + mobile-cols (2 cols desktop, 1 on mobile)

                |> Grid
                    cols=2
                    mobile-cols=1
                    column-first=true
                    place-items=start
                    with-padding=false

                    1st

                    2nd

                    3rd

                    4th

                |> Pause

                _*Case 13:*_ custom style (light pink background)

                |> Grid
                    cols=3
                    place-items=start
                    style=background-color:#ffe4e6

                    Item 1

                    Item 2

                    Item 3

                    Item 4

                    Item 5

                    Item 6

                |> Pause

                _*Case 14:*_ 3 cols, 2 on mobile, mobile-cutoff=520, center-on-overflow=true (from >>_true_false_)

                |> Grid
                    cols=3
                    mobile-cols=2
                    mobile-cutoff=520
                    center-on-overflow=true

                    a. $0.9^2 < 0.9$

                    b. $\sqrt{0.01} = 0.1$

                    c. $\sqrt[2]{\up{0.8}\sqrt[3]{2}} = \sqrt[3]{\up{0.8}\sqrt[2]{2}}$

                    d. ${\sqrt{2} \over \up{0.55}2} = \sqrt{0.5}$

                    e. ${1 \over \sqrt{2}} = \sqrt{0.5}$

                    f. $2^{30} > 1000^3$

                    g. ${1 \over 0.95} > 1.05$

                    h. $(-1)^{101} = -1$

                    i. ${100 \over \up{0.5}99} < {101 \over \up{0.5}100}$

                |> Pause

                _*Case 15:*_ 3 cols, place-items=end (from >>_true_false_)

                |> Grid
                    cols=3
                    place-items=end

                    $ (-1)^1 = $

                    $ (-1) = $

                    $ -1 $

                    $ (-1)^2 = $

                    $ (-1)\times (-1) = $

                    $ 1 $

                    $ (-1)^3 = $

                    $ (-1)\times(-1)\times (-1) = $

                    $ -1 $

                    $ (-1)^4 = $

                    $ (-1)\times(-1)\times(-1)\times(-1) = $

                    $ 1 $

                    $ (-1)^5 = $

                    $ \,\,\,(-1)\times(-1)\times(-1)\times(-1)\times(-1) = $

                    $ -1 $

                |> Pause

                _*Case 16:*_ 2 cols, 1 on mobile, place-items=start, with-padding=false, column-first=true (from >>_evaluate_lambda_functions_)

                |> Grid
                    cols=2
                    mobile-cols=1
                    place-items=start
                    with-padding=false
                    column-first=true

                    i. $(\lambda u.u^3)(0.5)$

                    ii. $(u \ra u^2)(x + 1)$

                    iii. $(\lambda t.t - 1)(100) \cdot (\lambda t.t + 1)(100)$

                    iv. $(u \ra u^2)(a + b)$

                |> Pause

                _*Case 17:*_ same as Case 16, 4 items only (from >>_evaluate_lambda_functions_)

                |> Grid
                    cols=2
                    mobile-cols=1
                    place-items=start
                    with-padding=false
                    column-first=true

                    i. $0.5^3 = 0.125$

                    ii. $(x + 1)^2 = x^2 + 2x + 1$

                    iii. $(100 - 1) \cdot (100 + 1) = 9999$

                    iv. $(a + b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2$

                |> Pause

                _*Case 18:*_ 2 cols, 1 on mobile, center-on-overflow=true, place-items=start, with-padding=false, column-first=true (from >>_true_or_false_composition_questions_)

                |> Grid
                    cols=2
                    mobile-cols=1
                    center-on-overflow=true
                    place-items=start
                    with-padding=false
                    column-first=true

                    i. $f \circ g = (x \ra f(g(x)))$

                    ii. $g \circ f = (x \ra f(g(x)))$

                    iii. $f \circ g = (x \ra g(f(x)))$

                    iv. $g \circ f = (x \ra g(f(x)))$

                |> Pause

                _*Case 19:*_ 3 cols, 2 items in the final row, center-on-overflow=true

                |> Grid
                    cols=3
                    center-on-overflow=true

                    a. $0.9^2 < 0.9$

                    b. $\sqrt{0.01} = 0.1$

                    c. $\sqrt[2]{\up{0.8}\sqrt[3]{2}} = \sqrt[3]{\up{0.8}\sqrt[2]{2}}$

                    d. ${\sqrt{2} \over \up{0.55}2} = \sqrt{0.5}$

                    e. ${1 \over \sqrt{2}} = \sqrt{0.5}$

                    f. $2^{30} > 1000^3$

                    g. ${1 \over 0.95} > 1.05$

                    h. $(-1)^{101} = -1$

        |> Exercise
            handle=mathblock-component

            |> ExerciseStatement
                What is the MathBlock component ?

            |> Solution
                _*MathBlock*_ renders a centered display equation, written with the `$$ ... $$` delimiters (as opposed to inline `$ ... $` math). It is responsive and has four behaviors worth understanding:

                |> Pause

                _*1. Centered & lazy.*_ The block is horizontally centered in the column. MathJax only typesets it once it scrolls near the viewport, then it fades in (opacity transition).

                |> Pause

                _*2. Auto scale-to-fit.*_ On mount and on every window resize, it measures the equation's natural width. If that width is wider than the available column ($innerWidth - 40px$), it automatically shrinks the whole block to fit the screen — so wide equations never overflow horizontally on narrow/mobile screens.

                |> Pause

                _*3. Click to toggle.*_ Clicking the block toggles between the fitted (scaled-down) size and its full natural size. Click a shrunk equation to see it at 100% (it may then overflow), and click again to re-fit it.

                |> Pause

                _*4. Resize recompute.*_ Whenever the window width changes, the fit decision is recomputed, so the same block looks right on desktop, tablet, and phone. _Try resizing your browser while watching the cases below._

                |> Pause

                _*Case 1:*_ short equation — narrower than the column, so it stays at natural size on every screen and clicking has no visible effect.

                $$
                2 \times 2 = 4
                $$

                |> Pause

                _*Case 2:*_ moderately wide equation — fits on desktop, but auto-shrinks once the window gets narrow enough. Resize to watch it shrink, or click to toggle full size.

                $$
                \sqrt{x^2 + 2x + 1} \rt{0.1} = \rt{0.1} \sqrt{(x + 1)^2} \rt{0.1} = \rt{0.1} |x + 1|
                $$

                |> Pause

                _*Case 3:*_ very wide equation — overflows the column on almost any screen, so it renders scaled-down by default. Click it to see the full-size version.

                $$
                (a + b + c + d + e)^2 = a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2 + e^2 + 2ab + 2ac + 2ad + 2ae + 2bc + 2bd + 2be + 2cd + 2ce + 2de
                $$

                |> Pause

                _*Case 4:*_ tall equation (stacked fraction) — height, not width, is the constraint here; it stays centered and fades in like the others.

                $$
                x = {-b \pm \sqrt{\up{0.6} b^2 - 4ac} \over \up{0.6} 2a}
                $$

        |> Exercise
            handle=side-image-component

            |> ExerciseStatement
                What is the SideImage component ?

            |> Solution
                _*Book-context fixtures.*_ These are the
                active clouds from ch1, ch2, bt1, and bt2.
                Each source excerpt keeps the actual attached
                paragraph/equation/image and its adjacent
                source blocks.

                |> Pause

                _*ch1.1: `svg_ch1_ch_minus_two_squared_cloud.svg`*_

                while, of course,
                $$
                2 \times 2 = 4
                $$
                is positive as well. But $\sqrt{4}$
                is, by definition, the unique _nonnegative_
                solution to $x^2 = 4$. Hence,

                $$
                \sqrt{(-2)^2} = 2
                $$

                |> ImageRight
                    src=images/svg_ch1_ch_minus_two_squared_cloud.svg
                    at-least-as-wide=false
                    offset-x=5em

                and, in particular, it is _not_ true that

                $$
                \sqrt{x^{2}} \rt{0.1} = \rt{0.1} x
                $$

                |> Pause

                _*ch1.2: `c1_abCD_cloud.svg`*_

                A little more generally, one has such identities
                as

                $$
                (a + b)(C + D) \,=\, aC + bC + aD + bD
                $$

                |> ImageLeft
                    src=images/c1_abCD_cloud.svg
                    offset-x=-1em

                that come from multiplying every term of the first
                parenthesis with every term of the second
                parenthesis. Indeed,


                $$
                (a + b)(C + D) = (a + b)C + (a + b)D
                $$

                |> Pause

                _*ch1.3-4: `svg_ch1_ex_25.svg`, `svg_ch1_ex_26.svg`*_

                Since

                $$
                {1 \over n} - {1 \over n+1} = {1 \over n(n+1)}
                $$

                |> ImageRight
                    src=images/svg_ch1_ex_25.svg
                    offset-x=-4rem

                (as previously computed) is smaller than

                $$
                {1 \over n^2}
                $$

                |> ImageRight
                    offset-x=-4rem
                    src=images/svg_ch1_ex_26.svg

                we will compute the difference

                $$
                {1 \over n^2} - {1 \over n(n+1)}
                $$

                |> Pause

                _*ch1.5: `svg_ch1_ex_27.svg`*_

                to avoid a minus sign in the result.
                (Computing the second difference and having
                a minus sign does not constitute a mistake,
                however.) Having said this, the difference is:

                $$
                \begin{align*}
                {1 \over n^2} - {1 \over n(n+1)} &= {1 \over n^2} \cdot {n+1 \over n+1} - {1 \over n(n+1)} \cdot
                {n \over n}\\
                &= {n+1 \over n^2(n+1)} - {n \over n^2(n+1)}\up{1.5}\\
                &= {1 \over n^2(n+1)}\up{1.5}
                \end{align*}
                $$

                |> ImageRight
                    src=images/svg_ch1_ex_27.svg
                    offset-y=-0.5em

                which, for large $n$, is roughly

                $$
                {1 \over n^3}
                $$

                |> Pause

                _*ch1.6-7: `true_false_sixth_root_of_two_cloud1.svg`, `true_false_sixth_root_of_two_cloud2.svg`*_

                $\sqrt[2]{\up{0.75}\sqrt[3]{2}}$
                and $\sqrt[3]{\up{0.75}\sqrt[2]{2}}$ are
                both equal to $\sqrt[6]{\up{0.6}2}$. To
                convince yourself, note that

                $$
                \begin{align*}
                &\,\, (\sqrt[2]{\up{0.75}\sqrt[3]{2}}\rt{0.1})^6 \\
                =&\,\, \up{1.4} \sqrt[2]{\up{0.75}\sqrt[3]{2}} \times \sqrt[2]{\up{0.75}\sqrt[3]{2}} \times \sqrt[2]{\up{0.75}\sqrt[3]{2}} \times \sqrt[2]{\up{0.75}\sqrt[3]{2}} \times \sqrt[2]{\up{0.75}\sqrt[3]{2}} \times \sqrt[2]{\up{0.75}\sqrt[3]{2}}\qquad\\
                =&\,\, \up{1.4} (\gbk\sqrt[2]{\up{0.75}\sqrt[3]{2}} \times \sqrt[2]{\up{0.75}\sqrt[3]{2}}\rt{0.11}) \times (\gbk\sqrt[2]{\up{0.75}\sqrt[3]{2}} \times \sqrt[2]{\up{0.75}\sqrt[3]{2}}\rt{0.11}) \times (\gbk\sqrt[2]{\up{0.75}\sqrt[3]{2}} \times \sqrt[2]{\up{0.75}\sqrt[3]{2}}\rt{0.11}) \\
                =& \,\, \up{1.4} (\sqrt[3]{\up{0.64}2}\rt{0.1}) \times (\sqrt[3]{\up{0.64}2}\rt{0.1}) \times (\sqrt[3]{\up{0.64}2}\rt{0.1})\\
                =& \,\, \up{1.4} 2
                \end{align*}
                $$

                |> ImageLeft
                    src=images/true_false_sixth_root_of_two_cloud1.svg
                    offset-y=23.5%

                and

                $$
                \begin{align*}
                \    &\,\, (\sqrt[3]{\up{0.75}\sqrt[2]{2}}\rt{0.1})^6 \\
                \   =&\,\, \up{1.4} \sqrt[3]{\up{0.75}\sqrt[2]{2}} \times \sqrt[3]{\up{0.75}\sqrt[2]{2}} \times \sqrt[3]{\up{0.75}\sqrt[2]{2}} \times \sqrt[3]{\up{0.75}\sqrt[2]{2}} \times \sqrt[3]{\up{0.75}\sqrt[2]{2}} \times \sqrt[3]{\up{0.75}\sqrt[2]{2}}\\
                \   =&\,\, \up{1.4} (\gbk\sqrt[3]{\up{0.75}\sqrt[2]{2}} \times \sqrt[3]{\up{0.75}\sqrt[2]{2}} \times \sqrt[3]{\up{0.75}\sqrt[2]{2}}\rt{0.11}) \times (\gbk\sqrt[3]{\up{0.75}\sqrt[2]{2}} \times \sqrt[3]{\up{0.75}\sqrt[2]{2}} \times \sqrt[3]{\up{0.75}\sqrt[2]{2}}\rt{0.11})\\
                \   =&\,\, \up{1.4} \sqrt[2]{\up{0.65}2} \times \sqrt[2]{\up{0.65}2}\\
                \   =&\,\, \up{1.4} 2
                \end{align*}
                $$

                |> ImageLeft
                    src=images/true_false_sixth_root_of_two_cloud2.svg
                    offset-y=23.5%

                so $(\sqrt[2]{\up{0.76}\sqrt[3]{2}}\rt{0.1})^6 =
                (\sqrt[3]{\up{0.76}\sqrt[2]{2}}\rt{0.1})^6 = 2$.

                Technically, however, a number $x$ such that

                $$
                x^6 = 2
                $$

                |> Pause

                _*ch1.8: `svg_ch1_ex_24.svg`*_

                of the sum? (By the way, this $n$-th term
                is the difference
                ${1 \over 99} - {1 \over 100}$ for $n = 99$,
                which is how we came to be reminded of this
                infinite sum in the first place.) Well...

                $$
                \begin{align*}
                {1 \over n} - {1 \over n+1} &= {1 \over n}\cdot{n+1 \over n+1}\, - \,
                {1 \over n+1}\cdot{n \over n}\up{1.5}\\
                &= {n+1 \over n(n+1)} - {n \over n(n+1)}\up{1.5}\\
                &= {1 \over n(n+1)}\up{1.5}
                \end{align*}
                $$

                |> ImageRight
                    src=images/svg_ch1_ex_24.svg
                    offset-y=-0.5em

                ...it's that much. (For example,

                $$
                {1 \over 1} - {1 \over 2} = {1 \over 1 \cdot 2} = {1 \over 2}
                $$

                |> Pause

                _*ch2.1: `c2_slope_formula_cloud.svg`*_

                are the horizontal and the vertical change,
                respectively, from $A$ to $B$, so

                $$
                \te{slope} = {y_2 - y_1 \over x_2 - x_1}
                $$

                |> ImageRight
                    src=images/c2_slope_formula_cloud.svg
                    offset-y=-0.7em
                    offset-x=3em

                more succinctly. We call this equation the _slope formula_.

                |> Pause

                _*ch2.2-3: `c2_swap_cloud.svg`, `tmp-images/c2_a_b_fraction_cloud.svg`*_

                In fact, we _should_ be able to algebraically
                verify that the slope formula gives the same
                answer if $(x_1, y_1)$ and $(x_2, y_2)$ swap
                places, or, namely, to show that the fractions

                |> ImageRight
                    src=images/c2_swap_cloud.svg
                    line=3
                    offset-x=-0.5em

                $$ {y_2 - y_1 \over x_2 - x_1}\qquad\,\,\,\,\,\te{and}\,\,\,\,\,\qquad{y_1 - y_2 \over x_1 - x_2} $$

                are somehow equal. But, indeed,

                $$ {y_2 - y_1 \over x_2 - x_1} = {-(y_2 - y_1) \over -(x_2 - x_1)} = {y_1 - y_2 \over x_1 - x_2} $$

                |> ImageLeft
                    src=images/c2_a_b_fraction_cloud.svg
                    offset-x=2.5em
                    offset-y=0

                which verifies this hypothesis. In particular,

                |> Pause

                _*ch2.4: `svg_ch2_ch_vertical_line_equation.svg`*_

                what would $a$ be equal to? Instead, a vertical
                line is described by an equation of the form

                $$x = c$$

                (see Fig. 1) where $c \in \rr$ is a constant,
                similarly to the more familiar equation

                |> ImageLeft
                    src=images/svg_ch2_ch_vertical_line_equation.svg
                    offset-y=-6em
                    offset-x= 1em

                $$y = b$$

                for a horizontal line, where $b \in \rr$ is a

                |> Pause

                _*ch2.5: `svg_3_plus_minus_one_times_x_cloud.svg`*_

                One should also bear in mind that an equation can
                define a line without having either of the forms
                “$y = ax + b$” or “$x = c$”. For example,

                $$x + y = 3$$

                is equivalent to

                $$y = 3 - x$$

                and thus describes a line of $y$-intercept $3$
                and slope $-1$.

                |> ImageRight
                    src=images/svg_3_plus_minus_one_times_x_cloud.svg
                    line=1

                |> Pause

                _*ch2.6: `c2_units_cloud.svg`*_

                The amount of time required
                for the balloon to go up by (say) $4$m is

                $$
                \frac{4\te{m}}{0.75\te{m}/\te{s}}\! = 5.3333...\te{s}
                $$

                |> ImageRight
                    src=images/c2_units_cloud.svg

                following the template

                $$
                \rt{0.1}
                \te{amount of time} \,\,=\,\, \frac{\!\rt{0.15}\te{amount of change}\!\rt{0.1}}{\te{rate of change}}
                \rt{0.1}
                $$

                |> Pause

                _*ch2.7-8: `c2_slope_two_and_minus_one_half_cloud_right.svg`, `c2_slope_two_and_minus_one_half_cloud_left.svg`*_

                This is true, as illustrated by the following
                pair of lines:

                |> Image
                    src=images/c2_slope_two_and_minus_one_half.svg
                    class=relative w-fit

                |> ImageRight
                    src=images/c2_slope_two_and_minus_one_half_cloud_right.svg
                    offset-y=20%
                    offset-x=-5%

                |> ImageLeft
                    src=images/c2_slope_two_and_minus_one_half_cloud_left.svg
                    offset-x=-25%
                    offset-y=47%

                In more detail, the two triangles are related
                by a $90^\circ$ rotation and so, likewise,
                are the lines defined by their hypotenuses!

                |> Pause

                _*ch2.9: `svg_ch2_ex_x0_y0_line_equation_cloud.svg`*_

                A point $(x,y) \ne (x_0,y_0)$ is on the
                line of slope $p$ if and only if

                $$ p = {y - y_0 \over x - x_0} $$

                |> ImageLeft
                    src=images/svg_ch2_ex_x0_y0_line_equation_cloud.svg
                    offset-x=0rem

                because

                $$ \,\,{y - y_0 \over x - x_0} $$

                |> Pause

                _*ch2.10: `svg_slope_times_run_equals_rise_cloud.svg`*_

                which is satisfied by the point $(x,y) = (x_0,y_0)$
                as well as by every other point on the line.
                This can be a final answer, and, pleasingly,
                has the form

                $$
                \te{“}\te{slope} \times \te{run} = \te{rise}\te{”}
                $$

                |> ImageRight
                    src=images/svg_slope_times_run_equals_rise_cloud.svg
                    offset-x=2.5rem
                    offset-y=0%

                which can also make it easy to remember!

                |> Pause

                _Note 1._
                The answer we gave is more often written

                $$
                y - y_0 = p(x - x_0)
                $$

                |> Pause

                _*bt1.1: `svg_bt1_bt_empty_set_cloud.svg`*_

                Mind you, concerning this example, that

                $$
                \Large \{\{\} \} \ne \{\}
                $$

                |> ImageRight
                    src=images/svg_bt1_bt_empty_set_cloud.svg
                    offset-x=3em

                because a box containing an empty box is not the
                same thing as an empty box! Specifically,

                $$
                \Large \{ \{\} \}
                $$

                |> Pause

                _*bt2.1: `svg_bt2_bt_alert_cloud.svg`*_

                For example,
                $$
                \Large 10^4 = 1 \times 10 \times 10 \times 10 \times 10 = 10000
                $$
                $$
                \Large 10^3 = 1 \times 10 \times 10 \times 10 = 1000
                $$
                $$
                \Large 10^2 = 1 \times 10 \times 10 = 100
                $$
                $$
                \Large 10^1 = 1 \times 10 = 10
                $$

                $$
                \Large 10^0 = 1 = 1
                $$

                |> ImageLeft
                    src=images/svg_bt2_bt_alert_cloud.svg
                    offset-y=-0.2em
                    offset-x= 4em

                (where, in the last line, $1$ is multiplied by $10$ _zero times_,
                as per the exponent, which is zero) by the first definition, while
                $$
                \Large 10^{-1} = 1\,/\,10 = 0.1
                $$
                $$
                \Large 10^{-2} = (1\,/\, 10)\,/\,10 = 0.01
                $$
                $$
                \Large 10^{-3} = ((1\,/\, 10)\,/\,10)\,/\,10 = 0.001
                $$
                $$
                \Large 10^{-4} = (((1\,/\, 10)\,/\,10)\,/\, 10)\,/\, 10 = 0.0001
                $$
                by the second definition.

                |> Pause

                _*bt2.2: `svg_bt2_bt_10_to_the_minus_0_cloud.svg`*_

                As $n$ successive divisions
                by $10$ is the same as one division by $10^n$, one also has

                |> MathBlock
                    \Large 10^{-n} = {1 \over 10^{\hspace{0.2ex}n}}\tag{A}

                for every positive integer $n$, which gives an alternate means of computing $10^{-n}$.
                Moreover, (>>_10_to_the_minus_n_) actually holds for

                __every__

                integer $n$, which is mildly important. In more
                detail, (>>_10_to_the_minus_n_) holds for $n = 0$ by inspection, and (>>_10_to_the_minus_n_)
                is equivalent to the identity

                |> ImageLeft
                    src=images/svg_bt2_bt_10_to_the_minus_0_cloud.svg
                    line=2

                |> MathBlock
                    \Large 10^{-n}10^n = 1 \tag{AA}

                which holds for $n$ if and only if it holds for
                $-n$. (By which we mean: replacing “$n$” by “$-n$”
                in (>>_10_to_the_minus_n__reworked) lands you right back on (>>_10_to_the_minus_n__reworked), due to the fact
                that $-{(-n)} = n$.) (So, namely, if (>>_10_to_the_minus_n__reworked) holds for all
                positive values of $\hspace{0.05em}n$, then it holds
                for all negative values of $n$, as well.)

                |> Pause

                _*bt2.3-5: `svg_bt2_bt_ten_to_the_3_times_ten_to_the_6_cloud.svg`, `svg_bt2_bt_ten_to_the_3_times_ten_to_the_9_cloud.svg`, `svg_bt2_bt_ten_to_the_6_times_ten_to_the_6_cloud.svg`*_

                __one million is a thousand thousand__
                because
                $$
                \Large 1000 \times 1000 = 1000\hspace{0.3ex}000
                $$
                by counting zeroes, or, equivalently, because
                $$
                \Large 10^3 \times 10^3 = 10^6
                $$
                by additivity of exponents. Similarly, note that

                __one billion is a thousand million__

                |> ImageLeft
                    src=images/svg_bt2_bt_ten_to_the_3_times_ten_to_the_6_cloud.svg

                and __one trillion is a thousand billion__

                |> ImageLeft
                    src=images/svg_bt2_bt_ten_to_the_3_times_ten_to_the_9_cloud.svg

                and also (while we're at it)

                __one trillion is a million million__

                |> ImageRight
                    src=images/svg_bt2_bt_ten_to_the_6_times_ten_to_the_6_cloud.svg

                as can be seen, for example, by replacing “billion”

                |> Pause

                _*bt2.6: `svg_bt2_bt_ten_to_the_minus_5_cloud.svg`*_

                Since
                $$
                \Large 0.00001 = 10^{-5}
                $$

                |> ImageRight
                    src=images/svg_bt2_bt_ten_to_the_minus_5_cloud.svg
                    offset-x= 0em

                the logarithm base $10$ of $0.00001$ is $-5$.

                |> Pause

                _*bt2.7: `svg_bt2_ex_equation_addition_cloud.svg`*_

                $$
                \Large a + b - c - 3 = 0
                $$
                $$
                \Large -a -2b - c = 0
                $$
                ...are satisfied! Adding the second and
                third equation together, we find
                $$
                \Large -b -2c -3 = 0
                $$
                |> ImageRight
                    src=images/svg_bt2_ex_equation_addition_cloud.svg
                    offset-y=-0.5em
                    offset-x= 0.5em
                and adding this last equation to the first
                equation above, we find
                $$
                \Large -c -2 = 0
                $$

                |> Pause

